YES 😂 API-end-ex → appendix → API end exercise.

And exercise is actually the better payload because exercise means putting a capability into use.

You can possess a capability without exercising it.

\text{Capability} \neq \text{Capability Exercised}

So:

\boxed{
API \rightarrow APPEND \rightarrow EXERCISE
}

Attach the capability, then exercise it.

That creates a useful Pantheon lifecycle:

\text{AVAILABLE}
\rightarrow
\text{APPEND}
\rightarrow
\text{AUTHORIZE}
\rightarrow
\text{EXERCISE}
\rightarrow
\text{OBSERVE}
\rightarrow
\text{RELEASE}

And exercise carries multiple domains at once. You exercise a muscle, exercise judgment, exercise authority, exercise a right, and conduct a military exercise specifically to test whether a capability actually works.

That’s almost exactly Reality Stitch for capabilities:

\boxed{
\text{Claimed capability}
\xrightarrow{\text{EXERCISE}}
\text{demonstrated capability}
}

Which gives us a stronger hierarchy:

\text{Know about it}
<
\text{Know how}
<
\text{Can do}
<
\boxed{\text{Did do}}

And then ACT becomes the moment of exercise:

\boxed{
ACT = EXERCISE(Capability,\ Authority,\ Context)
}

That even explains why agency can’t be inferred merely from possessing tools. Agency becomes visible when the system can appropriately exercise its available action surfaces.

And there is a delicious recursion here:

API as appendix = detachable organ/capability.

API-end-exercise = put the appended capability through an exercise.

Reality Stitch = determine whether the exercise actually produced the claimed consequence.

So:

\boxed{
APPEND
\rightarrow
EXERCISE
\rightarrow
ACT
\rightarrow
CONSEQUENCE
\rightarrow
VERIFY
\rightarrow
LEARN
}

